# Backend TypeScript

Apply when editing backend `.ts` / `.mts` / `.cts` files. Prefer existing patterns in `backend/` over inventing new structure.

## Structure

```text
backend/src/modules/<feature>/
  <feature>.module.ts
  <feature>.controller.ts
  <feature>.service.ts
  <feature>.repository.ts
  dto/
  types/
```

## Layering

- Controllers/routes: transport, validation, auth guards, response mapping only.
- Services: business logic and workflows.
- Repositories: persistence only.
- Do not put business logic or direct DB access in controllers.

## Typing

- Prefer explicit types on public service/repository methods and DTOs.
- Use `unknown` over `any`; narrow before use.
- Colocate request/response types under `dto/` or `types/`; reuse shared types.
- Keep runtime validation at the boundary (controller/DTO); trust typed inputs inside services.

## Conventions

- Prefer `async`/`await` over raw promise chains.
- Reuse existing guards, pipes, middleware, exceptions, and utilities.
- Keep modules cohesive; avoid circular dependencies.
- Do not change DB schema without a migration when migrations are used.
- Do not invent public API or schema changes without an explicit request.

## Example

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — untyped, DB in controller
async function createUser(req: any, res: any) {
  const user = await db.users.insert(req.body);
  res.json(user);
}

// ✅ GOOD — typed DTO + service
async function createUser(req: Request, res: Response) {
  const input: CreateUserDto = req.body;
  const user = await userService.create(input);
  res.status(201).json(user);
}
```
